    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that four fair 6-sided dice won't all show the same number when rolled, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes:** \n   Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n   There are 6 possible outcomes for each die (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6). Since the dice are rolled independently, the number of ways to get all four dice to show the same number is:\n   \\[\n   6 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 = 6^2 = 36\n   \\]\n   This is because there are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6) and each die has 6 possible outcomes within that number.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of outcomes where the dice don't all show the same number:**\n   To find this, subtract the number of outcomes where all dice show the same number from the total number of outcomes:\n   \\[\n   1296 - 36 = 1260\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability:**\n   The probability that the four dice won't all show the same number is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1260}{1296}\n   \\]\n   We can simplify this fraction:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1260}{1296} = \\frac{175}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{175}{216}}\n\\]",
