      "text": "To determine the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we first need to calculate the total number of possible outcomes and the number of favorable outcomes where all dice show the same number.\n\n1. Calculate the total number of possible outcomes:\nEach die has 6 faces, so the total number of outcomes when rolling four dice is:\n\\[\n6^4 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 1296\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate the number of outcomes where all dice show the same number:\nThere are 6 possible outcomes where all four dice show the same number (all showing 1, all showing 2, all showing 3, all showing 4, all showing 5, or all showing 6). So, there are 6 favorable outcomes.\n\n3. Calculate the probability:\nThe probability that all four dice do not all show the same number is the complement of the probability that they do all show the same number. Therefore, the probability is:\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = 1 - \\frac{6}{1296} = 1 - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{215}{216}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
