      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can use the complementary probability method.\n\nFirst, we find the probability that all four dice show the same number. Since the dice are fair, each face has an equal probability of appearing, which is 1/6. So, there are only two outcomes for this event: all dice showing 1 or all dice showing 6.\n\nThe probability of all four dice showing the same number is:\n\nP(all same) = P(all dice showing 1) + P(all dice showing 6) = (1/6) + (1/6) = 1/3\n\nNow, we consider the complementary event, in which all four dice show different numbers. There are 6 different numbers on a 6-sided die, so if we have four dice, there are 6 * 5 * 4 * 3 = 360 possible combinations for the different numbers.\n\nThe probability of all four dice showing different numbers is:\n\nP(all different) = 360/36 = 10/3\n\nNow, we use the complementary probability to find the probability that they won't all show the same number. This is the sum of the probability that they show different numbers and the probability that they show the same number:\n\nP(all different or same) = P(all different) + P(all same) = 10/3 + 1/3 = 11/3\n\nSo, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is 11/3.",
